    "message": "fix(routing): step over a rate-limited provider instead of waiting on it\n\nA provider serving a 429 cooldown does not refuse the next request. It\naccepts it and sleeps inside its own limiter until either the cooldown\nexpires -- up to an hour, when the upstream sends Retry-After -- or the\nattempt's deadline does, and only then hands the chain a timeout. With a\nfallback chain configured that wait is pure loss: the answer was one hop\naway the whole time.\n\n`throttle_remaining()` already reported exactly this cooldown on every\nprovider, and nothing above the provider layer looked at it: credential\nrotation used it to pick a key, routing did not use it at all.\n\nRouting now asks before committing an attempt, and only while another\ncandidate remains -- the last model on a route is tried cooldown or not,\nbecause skipping a bad model is an optimisation and refusing to try\nanything is an outage. A stepped-over model is recorded as `skipped` /\n`cooldown` in the attempt ledger rather than `failed`, and is not\ncounted toward ejection: it did not fail, the chain declined to wait.\n\n`RotatingProvider` inherited `BaseProvider`'s unconditional 0.0, so a\nmulti-credential provider claimed to be free with every key limited. It\nnow reports the shortest cooldown across its credentials, matching the\nbest case rotation would actually pick.\n\nNOT CREDITED WITH THE REPORTED FAILURE. This was written from the code\npath, before the reporter's request log was consulted. Their live log\n(161,812 rows) shows the reported \"exceeded the 600s request budget\" is\na different mechanism: the primary streams *thinking* deltas -- which\ncount as content, so the stall guard never fires -- for the full budget\nwhile producing no answer text, having committed at attempt 0. Their\n429s are rare and last occurred 2026-08-14. This change is a real gap\nin its own right and is not the fix for that report.\n\nVerification: 4 routing tests + 3 rotation tests, whole-file runs. Six\nmutations, all caught -- dropping the last-candidate guard, never\nskipping, benching the skipped model, min->max and min->0.0 on the\nrotating aggregate, and the ledger's error_kind. Full suite 5004 passed\nwith the 8 pre-existing PowerShell installer failures unchanged from\nfork/main (they detect the running server and take the deferred path).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
